#use(std/code)
#use(std/math: asciimath)

#section{Scripting}::

Schedulers embed in two hosts. From Python, compute the airmass yourself —
the code body is raw, so #tt{##}, braces and comments are all literal:

#code(lang: "python")
	import math

	def airmass(alt_deg): # Pickering (2002)
		h = math.radians(alt_deg)
		return 1 / math.sin(h + math.radians(244 / (165 + 47 * alt_deg ** 1.1)))

The Godot companion app drives the mount from GDScript:

#code(lang: "gdscript")
	extends Node

	signal slew_finished(target)

	func slew(target: String) -> void:
		print("slewing to %s" % target)
		emit_signal("slew_finished", target)

Pass #code{--dry-run} to print the plan without moving the dish.

The airmass at altitude #m{h} is roughly #m{1/sin(h)}; the scheduler uses
the full form:

#math
	X = 1/(sin(h + 244/(165 + 47 h^(1.1))))

#section{Units}::

Altitude #m{h} is in degrees throughout; airmass #m{X} is dimensionless.
This subsection also shows the index toctree's #tt{depth: 2} doing real
work: it is pruned from the multi-file nav (level 3) but stays in #tt{##toc}.
